#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825} :activity/id #uuid "ccbd1570-797c-5564-8927-e502aa05caec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527 :topic/id #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" :topic/math-academy-id 3029} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163179 :tutorial/content "We can do the same trick using the **elementary column operations** instead of row operations.\n\nThere are three types of elementary *column* operations, which are entirely analogous to the corresponding row operations:\n\n- Switching any two columns: \n\n$$\nC_i \\leftrightarrow C_j\n$$\n\n- Multiplying (each element of) a column by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nC_i := k C_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column: \n\n$$\nC_j := C_j+k C_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** \n\n- Unlike elementary row operations, elementary *column* operations change the solutions of a system of linear equations. This is because column operations alter the coefficients associated with each variable across the system, changing the equations.\n\n- The exception is swapping two columns - this doesn't alter the solutions *provided that* we swap the corresponding variables in the solution vector. For example, if we swap the first and second columns, we must treat the second variable as the first and vice versa. The solution set remains unchanged because the relationships between the variables and their coefficients are preserved." :tutorial/id #uuid "e947134c-e909-5a3b-85a6-d284e04816e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10652 :tutorial/title "Column Operations"} :step/id #uuid "ce002306-ea12-5d28-8c26-a1710d2f4327" :step/math-academy-id 218771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163214 :tutorial/content "When studying linear algebra, it is common to use elementary row operations to reduce a matrix $A$ to another matrix $B$ such that $B$ contains many zeros. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the entry $a_{11}$ located in the top left-hand corner. We can reduce *all* of the entries *below* it using elementary row operations.\n\nFirst, we subtract $3$ times the first row from the second row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $5$ times the first row to the third row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! We have successfully performed elementary row operations to reduce all of the entries below $a_{11}$ to zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e0cae18-aa8d-5b1d-9aab-4a5334dec0c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4828 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b68078fb-1628-5a76-bb97-1c1261f6641d" :step/math-academy-id 30052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161965 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161757 :question/id #uuid "70991204-370a-5804-a04e-7e664db052de" :question/math-academy-id "e-8048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400478} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218640